From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:59:40 +0200
Subject: Add --progress-fd FD: machine-readable progress for a calling program

Walnut Games runs game-data-packager for the player and shows what it is doing. Today the only
progress is a percentage drawn on a terminal, and every failure is exit status 1 with English
text, so a caller can't tell "no files here" from "a version we don't know" from "needs a
download" without parsing log messages.

With --progress-fd FD, one line per event goes to FD (game_data_packager/progress_fd.py):

  step <text>           searching a path, unpacking or downloading a file, building a package
  file <name> <bytes>   a file being checked, identified or unpacked from an archive
  generated <path>      a package was built
  error <kind> <text>   once, before a failed run exits

The kind is decided where game-data-packager detects the failure: not-found (no package could
be completed and no file of a known name turned up), unknown-version (files of known names with
checksums it doesn't know, or a file given on the command line that matches nothing),
unsuitable (a known release that gdp itself refuses, text "<file> <release>: <reason>", e.g.
"hexen.wad Hexen 1.0: Hexen 1.1 (registered) is required", when nothing usable was found),
needs-download (a file only available by download, downloads off or failed), no-space (the
download space check, ENOSPC, or a file system we write to is full when the run fails), failed
(anything else, e.g. a missing unpacker). Without the option nothing changes: the normal output
on stdout and stderr is the same.

Forwarded: no

